## Names

Names are measured over six layers by [`notes/scripts/measure_names.py`](../scripts/measure_names.py) and recorded in [`notes/names.md`](../names.md): four title layers (family titles, manuscript directory names, the optional titles of statement environments, the `main_results` declaration names) and two body layers (manuscript prose with the bibliography stripped, and the author fields of the shipped `.bib` files). A name is a **subject** when a title layer or a declaration carries it and a **citation** when only prose or a bibliography does, so only subject names get a page: {{ n_name_pages }} pages in [`names/`](names/) over {{ n_name_rows }} measured names. The other {{ n_cite }} are bibliography surnames that never reach a title layer — rows in [`names.tsv`](names.tsv) with `role = citation`, not pages. {{ n_name_edges }} family-name edges cover {{ n_name_covered }} of {{ n_ledger }} families; {{ n_nofam }} names resolve to no family. Each page states, per source, which layer produced the hit: a name in a body paragraph is a citation, not a subject.

